    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_pyf_321",
      "name": "normalize-and-dedupe-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Equal after cleaning? Which row survives?",
      "description": "Predict what normalization and a dict-based dedupe really do before you write the cleaning function.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "import unicodedata\n\nnfc = \"Hà Nội\"                              # typed on a laptop (composed)\nnfd = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFD\", nfc)    # the same city from the mobile app\nnbsp = \"Hà\\u00a0Nội\"                       # pasted from a web page\n\nWhich expressions are True? (Select all that apply.)",
          "explanation": "NFC recombines the base letters and combining marks of nfd, so the first comparison is True. casefold() changes case only: nfd keeps its separate combining marks, so the casefolded strings still differ. strip() removes whitespace at the ends only, and the non-breaking space is in the middle, so nbsp.strip() is unchanged. split() with no argument splits on any whitespace, including the non-breaking space, and \" \".join(...) puts one normal space back, which gives exactly nfc. Normalization never removes accents: the casefolded city is \"hà nội\", not \"ha noi\" - that spelling needs an alias table.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", nfd) == nfc",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "nfd.casefold() == nfc.casefold()",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "nbsp.strip() == nfc",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "\" \".join(nbsp.split()) == nfc",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", nfc).casefold() == \"ha noi\"",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "rows = [\n    {\"email\": \"lan@lotus.vn\", \"city\": \"Huế\"},\n    {\"email\": \" Lan@Lotus.vn\", \"city\": \"Đà Nẵng\"},\n    {\"email\": \"minh@lotus.vn\", \"city\": \"Huế\"},\n]\ncustomers = {}\nfor row in rows:\n    customers[row[\"email\"].strip().casefold()] = row[\"city\"]\nprint(list(customers.items()))\n\nWhat does it print?",
          "explanation": "Both Lan rows have the same key \"lan@lotus.vn\" after strip() and casefold(). Assigning to a key that already exists replaces its value, so the second row's city wins: this loop keeps the LAST row for each customer. The key itself keeps its original position - a dict is ordered by when each key was first inserted, and replacing a value does not move it. To keep the first row instead, skip the row when the key is already in the dict (if key in customers: continue).",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "[('lan@lotus.vn', 'Huế'), ('minh@lotus.vn', 'Huế')]",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "[('lan@lotus.vn', 'Đà Nẵng'), ('minh@lotus.vn', 'Huế')]",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "[('minh@lotus.vn', 'Huế'), ('lan@lotus.vn', 'Đà Nẵng')]",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "[('lan@lotus.vn', 'Huế'), ('lan@lotus.vn', 'Đà Nẵng'), ('minh@lotus.vn', 'Huế')]",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
